Here's a very basic example of how a brute-force attack might be simulated in JavaScript for educational purposes. This example is not meant to be used for any unethical activities.

```javascript
function bruteForcePassword crackedPassword, maxLength) {
  const charset = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789';
  const charsetLength = charset.length;

  for (let length = 1; length <= maxLength; length++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < charsetLength ** length; i++) {
      let password = '';
      let tempI = i;
      for (let j = 0; j < length; j++) {
        password += charset[tempI % charsetLength];
        tempI = Math.floor(tempI / charsetLength);
      }
      if (password === crackedPassword) {
        console.log(`Password found: ${password}`);
        return password;
      }
    }
  }
  console.log('Password not found.');
}

// Example usage:
const crackedPassword = 'password123'; // The password you are trying to brute-force
const maxLength = 8; // The maximum length of the password
bruteForcePassword(crackedPassword, maxLength);
```

This script will attempt to brute-force a password of a given length, up to a specified maximum length. It uses a simple character set and will print the password if it finds a match.

Remember, this is a very basic example and real-world brute-forcing would involve much more complexity, including handling different character sets, dealing with case sensitivity, and potentially using more sophisticated algorithms to improve efficiency.

Always ensure you have permission to test the security of a system, and never engage in unauthorized testing or hacking.

