In C++, you can use a macro to run the same code inline on multiple named variables. Macros are defined using the `#define` directive. Here's an example of how you can do this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

#define PRINT_AND_INCREMENT(x) std::cout << (x) << " " << (x)++ << std::endl

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    PRINT_AND_INCREMENT(a);
    PRINT_AND_INCREMENT(b);
    PRINT_AND_INCREMENT(c);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the macro `PRINT_AND_INCREMENT` takes a variable `x` as an argument, prints its value, increments it, and then prints the incremented value. The macro is then used with different variables `a`, `b`, and `c`, allowing the same code to be run inline on multiple named variables.

